
Controller tests on the host
============================

Run from the repository root with PlatformIO Core and a host C++ compiler
(Apple Command Line Tools on macOS):

    pio test -e native

For the standard IDE installation, use ~/.platformio/penv/bin/pio instead of
pio if it is not on PATH. PlatformIO downloads its native platform and Unity
dependency on the first run. No CoreS3 is needed and this command never uploads.

The test_controller suite uses Unity. setUp() creates a fresh RobotStateMachine
value for each test; timestamps are explicit uint32_t millisecond values and
press inputs are individual events, not held-touch levels. No real sleeps or
hardware mocks are used. The native build_src_filter compiles the production
RobotStateMachine.cpp and TranscriptionProtocol.cpp; Arduino main,
RobotController, RobotAudio, RobotTranscription, FaceRenderer, and M5Unified
are excluded. The firmware builds the same helper and supplies millis() and
M5.Touch.getDetail().wasPressed() at its hardware boundary.

The 12 tests cover ten automatic capture/replay cycles, repeated busy presses,
held-event absence, completion races, stale completion facts, failures at each
active phase, and retries. Parameterized cases check 4999/5000 ms watchdog
boundaries (including simultaneous completion), playback handoff without a timer
extension, and completion/failure/timeout inactivity refresh across uint32_t wrap.
Idle boundaries, press precedence, three sleep/wake cycles, and initialization
at current uptime are covered. begin(now) now resets to idle and starts its clock
at now; public state setters were removed to keep audio and face state aligned.
Simulated FAILED inputs represent initialization, request, or stopped-driver
failures. They do not test M5Unified calls. See docs/plan/audio-record-replay.md.

The test_transcription suite exercises the same bounded HTTP parser used by
the worker, without a socket or RTOS mock. It covers numeric IPv4 base URLs,
fragmented multilingual UTF-8, empty/max-size results and embedded NUL lengths,
HTTP rejection/redirects, incomplete bodies, malformed or ambiguous framing,
media/encoding rejection, fixed header/line/body limits, canary-protected output,
and invalid/overlong/surrogate/out-of-range UTF-8. It does not establish socket
cleanup, task allocation, clip ownership under real concurrency, Wi-Fi memory
use or device timing. Those remain CoreS3 checks in the tap-to-transcribe plan.

Firmware compile check (also no upload):

    pio run -e m5stack-cores3

Native tests establish decisions and once-per-transition signals only; actual
driver request counts, microphone/speaker routing, audio quality, physical touch,
renderer/Serial notifications, and blink recovery require the audio plan's CoreS3
checks. Neither command above flashes a device; a build does not prove behavior.
